\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Hybrid random Clifford circuit. Brickwork layers of random two-qubit Clifford gates (blue boxes labeled C) alternate with single-site measurements (red M symbols); each qubit is measured independently with probability $p$ per layer, so the measurement locations shown are illustrative. Even layers pair qubits $(0,1),(2,3),(4,5)$; odd layers pair $(1,2),(3,4)$. The competition between unitary scrambling and projective measurement drives the entanglement crossover at $p \approx p_c$.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:schematic}{{1}{3}{Hybrid random Clifford circuit. Brickwork layers of random two-qubit Clifford gates (blue boxes labeled C) alternate with single-site measurements (red M symbols); each qubit is measured independently with probability $p$ per layer, so the measurement locations shown are illustrative. Even layers pair qubits $(0,1),(2,3),(4,5)$; odd layers pair $(1,2),(3,4)$. The competition between unitary scrambling and projective measurement drives the entanglement crossover at $p \approx p_c$}{figure.1}{}}
